name: arrays and strings
intro: An array is a fixed run of same-typed values, side by side in memory.
intro: Its size is part of the declaration, and you can ask C for it:
intro:
intro:     const char *days[] = {"mon", "tue", "wed"};
intro:     size_t n = sizeof days / sizeof days[0];   /* 3 */
intro:
intro: Bytes in the whole array divided by bytes in one element. Write
intro: that once and the count follows the data when you add a row. That
intro: count is a `size_t` (unsigned). Loop with `size_t i`, and cast before
intro: comparing it with a signed value: `k > (long)n`.
intro:
intro: C does not check indexes. `days[7]` compiles, runs, and reads
intro: whatever sits past the array -- undefined behaviour. It may print
intro: junk today and crash next week. The check is your job: compare
intro: the index to the count before you use it.
intro:
intro: A string is a char array ending in a NUL byte (`'\0'`). "hi" takes
intro: three bytes. `strlen` counts up to the NUL and stops, so it is 2;
intro: `sizeof` is the array's size, 3. Mix them up and you are off by
intro: one, which in C means a write past the end.
intro:
intro: Never use `gets` (it cannot be told a limit, so it was removed from
intro: C11), and treat `strcpy`, `strcat`, `sprintf` as the same trap.
intro: Use `snprintf`. It never writes past the size you give it, and it
intro: returns the length the result WOULD have had -- so you can tell
intro: when it was cut short:
intro:
intro:     char buf[16];
intro:     int n = snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, "%s-%d", name, id);
intro:     if (n < 0 || (size_t)n >= sizeof buf)
intro:         /* did not fit */
intro:
intro: `fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin)` reads one line, keeps the `\n`,
intro: and returns NULL at end of input. `<string.h>` has `strcmp` (0
intro: means equal), `strchr` (find a byte) and `memcpy` (copy n bytes).
---
q: dayname.c -- number to weekday
spec: Takes one argument, a number 1 to 7, and prints the weekday:
spec: `mon tue wed thu fri sat sun` (1 is `mon`), then a newline.
spec:
spec: Keep the names in an array and take the count from sizeof, not a
spec: typed 7. A number outside 1..7, or anything that is not a whole
spec: number, prints `dayname: want 1-7` to standard error and exits 1.
spec: Any other argument count prints nothing and exits 2.
file: dayname.c
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char *argv[])
prog: {
prog: 	static const char *const days[] = {
prog: 		"mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun",
prog: 	};
prog: 	const size_t n = sizeof days / sizeof days[0];
prog: 
prog: 	if (argc != 2)
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 
prog: 	char *end;
prog: 	errno = 0;
prog: 	long k = strtol(argv[1], &end, 10);
prog: 	if (errno != 0 || end == argv[1] || *end != '\0' || k < 1 || k > (long)n) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "dayname: want 1-%zu\n", n);
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	printf("%s\n", days[k - 1]);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: dayname 1
case: dayname 7
case: dayname 0 2>&1; echo $?
case: dayname 8 2>&1; echo $?
case: dayname x 2>&1; echo $?
case: dayname; echo $?
hint: days[k - 1] is the name for k; check k against 1 and the array count before you index
